WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.930
The Harry Potter books and films have been
combed through by everyone who wished they'd

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:07.750
gotten a Hogwart's acceptance letter on their
11th birthdays.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:13.040
But there are some details in the movies that
only die-hard Potter fans could really pluck

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.040
out.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.300
Here are some Harry Potter Easter eggs that
only bona fide members of Dumbledore's Army

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.470
would've caught.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.310
Snape's coded message

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.930
It took a long time for Harry Potter to figure
out that Professor Snape wasn't actually out

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:29.010
to get him and make his stay at Hogwarts miserable.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.160
If he was a star student of language like
his pal Hermione, though, he might've been

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:37.880
able to unlock the Half-Blood Prince's true
intentions long before he got a screening

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:42.050
of Snape's memories through Dumbledore's Pensieve
in the final chapter.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.140
In the Sorcerer's Stone, Snape challenges
Harry:

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:51.510
"Tell me, what would I get if I added powdered
root of asphodel to an infusion of wormwood?"

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:56.390
The moment seems like a check on Harry's sudden
star status as the big man on campus, but

00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:01.579
in retrospect, superfans have always found
a little more weight to those words.

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:07.569
See, asphodel is actually a member of the
lily family and can invoke the phrase "my

00:01:07.569 --> 00:01:09.679
regrets follow you to your grave".

00:01:09.679 --> 00:01:14.829
Meanwhile, wormwood is literally defined as
something "bitter or grievous."

00:01:14.829 --> 00:01:18.840
This has led many to believe that Snape was
really just telling Mister Potter that he

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.990
was bitterly regretful of his mother, Lily,
passing away.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:27.150
Given the fact that he loved her most of his
life and clutched her corpse in agony the

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.219
day he discovered The Boy Who Lived, that
makes a lot of sense, then, doesn't it?

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:34.259
So much seven

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:39.499
Harry Potter's number on the Gryffindor Quidditch
Team is seven, and it's no accident: he's

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:44.479
the seventh of Voldemort's horcruxes, after
all, and there are seven books in the series.

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:49.020
So the filmmakers went to painstaking efforts
to include the fabled numerology throughout

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.681
the series, whether you noticed it or not.

00:01:50.681 --> 00:01:55.530
There are a ton of things that just so happened
to add up to seven throughout the movies,

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:59.419
like the headcount on snakes that line the
door to the Chamber of Secrets, the number

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:04.139
of players on each Quidditch team, the number
of children in the Weasley family, and the

00:02:04.139 --> 00:02:08.630
number of Harry Potters that are made from
the Order of the Phoenix to disguise the real

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:09.730
deal.

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.900
Looks like seven really was Harry's lucky
number...

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.879
Deathly Hallows everywhere

00:02:14.879 --> 00:02:20.019
Speaking of visual iconography, the movies
also made a point of flashing the sign of

00:02:20.019 --> 00:02:23.019
the Deathly Hallows during pivotal scenes
as well.

00:02:23.019 --> 00:02:27.879
For example, in the Goblet of Fire, Dumbledore
gazes upon his collection of artifacts in

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:33.409
his office, and they're arranged in such a
way as to give us a wink to the triangle-circle-line

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:38.709
symbol that would represent the Cloak of Invisibility,
the Resurrection Stone, and the Elder Wand

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:40.400
later on in the story.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:45.540
Another example comes in Deathly Hallows:
Part 2, when Harry, feeling bad about Dobby's

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:49.439
death, looks in the shard of mirror the house
elf had in his possession.

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:55.319
The lens of his glasses and a streak of glare
create the symbol for a split second as well,

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:59.299
seemingly reminding him of his mission to
destroy Voldemort's horcruxes.

00:02:59.299 --> 00:03:04.060
Fans could launch a full-scale Easter egg
hunt to find all the other nods to this paramount

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.670
piece of symbolism because it's been scattered
throughout the film series in small moments

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.219
such as these.

00:03:09.219 --> 00:03:10.470
Trelawney's prophecies

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:16.920
Hermione Granger didn't care much for Divination,
but Professor Trelawney's prophecies did turn

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.270
out to be astonishingly accurate, even when
she seemed her kookiest.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.299
The one that most people point to as prescient
in retrospect is from the books.

00:03:25.299 --> 00:03:31.129
At one point, she declined a dinner invitation
in Prisoner of Azkaban because twelve people

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:33.769
were already seated and thirteen is unlucky.

00:03:33.769 --> 00:03:37.829
The theory then was that the first person
to rise from a table of 13 would be the first

00:03:37.829 --> 00:03:42.450
to die, and, as fate would have it, Sirius
Black stood first from a table of such number

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.510
in Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.810
And yes, he died first.

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:51.420
But it takes an especially keen sensibility
to ferret out that her birthday claim about

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.950
Harry was also ultimately on point, even though
it seemed way off at the time.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:01.120
In the same chapter in Prisoner of Azkaban,
she said she thought he was born in midwinter,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.629
but he answered that his birthday was actually
in July.

00:04:04.629 --> 00:04:09.170
However, Voldemort's birthday was New Year's
Eve, and since Harry had a piece of Voldemort

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:14.450
lodged in his soul after surviving the attack
on his family, well, she was kinda right then,

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:15.440
wasn't she?

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:17.880
Plus, of course, she totally called it when
she said...

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.540
The chocolate frog bookend

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.720
There's a small wink to Harry's first journey
on the Hogwarts Express contained in the very

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.470
last scene of Deathly Hallows: Part 2.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:51.600
During Harry's first ride, when he initially
befriended Ron Weasley, he'd used his new

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.570
pocket full of Gringotts galleons to buy "the
lot" of treats on the trolley.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.300
It didn't take long for he and Ron to stuff
their faces with all the bewitched goodies

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.640
they could stomach.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.320
One of the chocolate frogs, however, got away
and hopped through the window, which wasn't

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.040
so much of a problem for Ron since he was
more interested in collecting the character

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:10.850
cards that came with them anyway.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:16.190
In the final film, as Harry and Ginny's children
board the same train with Ron and Hermione's

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:21.200
kids, the chocolate frog returns to the window
as a nod to the start of a new story — this

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:26.480
time, with boy wizard Albus Severus Potter
unknowingly beginning his journey as the Cursed

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:27.840
Child.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.020
Voldemort was in Hogwarts the whole time

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:34.520
In the film version of Prisoner of Azkaban,
there's a moment when the students are walking

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.390
through the staircase when an unexpectedly
familiar face pops up in a portrait: Lord

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:40.390
Voldemort.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:45.140
It's a blurred and fairly quick pass-by situation,
but that's gotta be him–a Nosferatu-like

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:49.900
bald wizard in flowing black capes reaching
out to the students that pass him by in his

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:51.120
menacing way.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.120
If it seems odd that Voldemort's portrait
would be included in the halls of Hogwarts,

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.690
that's because it is odd.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:02.420
Not only was he dubbed "He-Who-Shall-Not-Be-Named"
by everyone and kept far, far away from the

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:06.940
mainstream wizarding community, but Ralph
Fiennes hadn't yet arrived as the character.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:11.530
It'd be a whole 'nother installment — The
Goblet of Fire — before he'd show up.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.360
Foreshadowing much?

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.680
Dial 6-2-4-4-2 for magic

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.720
In Order of the Phoenix, Harry is called to
the Ministry of Magic to have a disciplinary

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:25.770
hearing before the Wizengamot after producing
his Patronus to save himself and cousin Dudley

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:28.310
from a Dementor while in the Muggle realm.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:32.800
Mr. Weasley accompanies Harry to the hearing
and takes Harry into a telephone box to get

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:34.210
access to the Ministry.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:38.200
In the book, he narrates the number he's dialing
to get through as 6-2-4-4-2.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:43.740
On a traditional telephone like that one,
the letters would be arranged on the keypad

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.300
as well and those digits just so happen to
spell "M-A-G-I-C."

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:49.940
There's no chance that was a coincidence.

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:51.430
Hedwig's theme

00:06:51.430 --> 00:06:55.770
The score to the Harry Potter film series
has become quite an earworm since the films

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:59.440
were released, unmistakably belonging to the
series forevermore.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.020
The introductory music has been titled "Hedwig's
Theme," but the character who actually played

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:06.080
the music during the movie was Hagrid.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:10.240
In the first film, he plays the melody on
his flute as Harry, Ron, and Hermione all

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:13.970
rush up to him for critical information on
their latest meanderings around the school

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:14.970
grounds.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:18.050
Maybe it should've been called "Hagrid's Theme"
instead?

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.140
Aberforth's bar

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:23.190
There's a scene in The Order of the Phoenix
when Harry, Ron, and Hermione duck out of

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:26.700
Hogwarts to formulate Dumbledore's Army at
Hogsmeade.

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:30.640
When they hit up the Hog's Head, a local bar,
for some private planning, they find that

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:34.910
there's just one other person there: a bartender
lurking in the dark.

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.150
And he's got a goat.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.150
Only J.K.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:41.180
Rowling's most faithful DA members would have
recognized that this was an early glimpse

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.880
of Albus Dumbledore's brother, Aberforth...who
was obsessed with goats.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:47.000
And owned a bar.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:51.160
That establishment would later come into play
again in Deathly Hallows: Part 2 when the

00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:55.860
trio tries to sneak back into Hogwarts during
another troublesome Headmaster switcheroo

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:00.140
moment–this time, with Aberforth arriving
to lend a hand.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:02.050
Opening at the close

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:07.050
There's a lot that can be read into the Snitch's
inscription that so famously read "I open

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:08.120
at the close."

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.860
The most obvious interpretation is that, when
Harry kisses it in Deathly Hallows, it opens

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:17.580
itself up to present him with the Resurrection
Stone so he can have a pep talk with his long-lost

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.770
loved ones before sacrificing himself to Voldemort
to fulfill his part of the prophecy.

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:26.720
He got it in the first book, when he started
this journey, and it opened at the close.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:27.890
Simple enough, right?

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:31.960
What should really get the eyebrows raising,
though, is the fact that Rowling published

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.690
the first book back in 1997, when the events
of Deathly Hallows took place.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:41.440
So, it's also a statement that symbolizes
the entire franchise as a whole.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.729
It literally opens at the close.

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:46.270
Is this all in Harry's head?

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:50.500
One of the grimmest fan theories associated
with the Harry Potter books and films is the

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:55.770
idea that everything magical about Harry's
life outside of 4 Privet Drive was just a

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.890
cruel fiction of his overactive imagination.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:03.300
The idea behind this one is that Harry, who
was a tortured orphan and was made to live

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:07.950
in the closet under the stairs by his cruel
aunt and uncle, created this fictional reality

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.630
as a coping mechanism for his circumstances.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:16.170
And in the final installment, there's a momentary
wink to that very possibility — and a possible

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:19.450
acknowledgment that it may be right on the
money after all.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:24.080
After Harry has died by Voldemort's wand and
wakes up in a dreamy version of King's Cross

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:28.490
Station, he meets with Dumbledore, who gives
him the option to stay in the living realm

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:30.050
or go onto the beyond.

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:34.560
Harry asks Dumbledore if this is in his head
and Dumbledore answers like so:

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.000
"Is this all real, or is it just happening
inside my head?"

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.500
"Of course it’s happening inside your head,
Harry.

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:43.250
Why should that mean that it's not real?"

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:48.460
To fans looking for confirmation of that theory,
there's really only one possible response:

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.730
"And bloody hell!"

00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:51.960
Thanks for watching!

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:54.710
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:56.810
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

